ef 17592186485227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9365ada-91e3-54da-a0d9-612ba60324bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df655b6c-59fb-5622-9ea9-612d1e33b110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90%$ of those infected and $9%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10d543bf-463f-57f9-8fa9-461f96d806af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who doesn't eat after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8763b60a-8a9e-5a4f-9933-1a229bcc9ffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't eat after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|N)=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|M)=0.05,\\,P(N|{M}^{′})=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|N) & =\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.49)}{(0.05)(0.49)+(0.32)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈13%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $98%$ of allergy sufferers and $4%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $2%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93a07e59-f865-5d60-89bb-f9fb0b96134d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy  testing positive is $98%,$ and the probability of a  non-allergic person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.98,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $2%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.02,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.98)(0.02)}{(0.98)(0.02)+(0.04)(0.98)} \\\\  & ≈0.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1071 "Two identical bins contain chips. The first bin contains four green and two yellow chips. The second bin has three green and one yellow chip. A bin is chosen at random, and a chip is selected from the chosen bin, also at random. If we know that the selected chip is green, what is the probability that it came from the second bin?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9db6fca8-e341-510d-8f9f-24e493c62cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that the second bin is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected chip is green. Then, we require $P(S|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(S|G)=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${S}^{′}$ is the event that the first bin is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first bin contains four green and two yellow chips, and the second bin contains three green and one yellow chip, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|S)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P(G|{S}^{′})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the bin is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=P({S}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|G) & =\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{17}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(E|G)=0.15,$ $P(E)=0.75,$ and $P(G)=0.3,$ compute $P(G|E).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0c5c51a-9ae7-508b-bfcb-a9d35170be46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G|E) & =\\frac{P(E|G)P(G)}{P(E)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.75} \\\\  & =0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular school, $65%$ of students attend a history course, and $55%$ of students attend a language course.  If $45%$ of language students attend a history course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen history student also attends a language course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2713c99-1478-532d-bcdd-d19b74d5beb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $L$ be the event that they attend a language course. Then, we require $P(L|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(L|H)=\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|L)=0.45$ since $45%$ of language students also attend a history course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.55$, since $55%$ of students attend a language course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|H) & =\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.45)(0.55)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular city, about $60%$ of mornings are cloudy and $30%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edf69e26-0d8e-540c-b142-6a409309f520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4\\cdot 0.3}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522019 #edb/ref 1080 "34%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02a70b8e-641b-5f9f-8429-083646503d5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522026 #edb/ref 1080 "0.48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522026 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522026 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04ab9885-0e91-5abb-84cb-80ed9e925c3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522032 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8}{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522032 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522032 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08668600-cdfa-52f8-ab7a-d4a36c8ad7e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522056 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522056 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522056 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "124a67f2-41f7-57f2-9556-2e058ea1059e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522068 #edb/ref 1080 "45%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522068 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522068 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "173c78ca-07cd-5a6c-9761-ea787464b15a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522073 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "187c8520-6d60-5a7b-a412-938adbc2ec03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522075 #edb/ref 1080 "56%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18a6a22b-b44c-52f9-ad3e-8ce36c0c0333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522091 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{9}{23}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522091 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522091 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ffc0230-8021-5fb9-97c8-3bdfd65c4744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522100 #edb/ref 1080 "31%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522100 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522100 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23c9b185-7787-54a4-bca2-f16375591efa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186